1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,360
hi, I'm Ankur Mehra.

2
00:00:01,414 --> 00:00:07,145
I have been working with, major US banks
for fifteen plus years, so I carry rich

3
00:00:07,145 --> 00:00:09,724
experience in financial risk technology.

4
00:00:10,402 --> 00:00:14,814
Today, I want to talk about, building
the resilient MLOps pipelines for

5
00:00:14,884 --> 00:00:17,365
illiquid loans portfolio valuation.

6
00:00:17,937 --> 00:00:19,393
So that's a mouthful.

7
00:00:19,704 --> 00:00:20,938
So here's the short version.

8
00:00:21,644 --> 00:00:27,688
When a financial institution value loans
that don't trade on an open market, like

9
00:00:27,688 --> 00:00:33,815
how a stock trades on an open market,
the model is only half the story.

10
00:00:34,437 --> 00:00:37,269
The other half is the pipeline around it.

11
00:00:37,798 --> 00:00:43,628
And my argument today is that reliability,
traceability, and reproducibility

12
00:00:43,724 --> 00:00:45,450
are core engineering requirements.

13
00:00:45,485 --> 00:00:51,290
They are not afterthoughts the
challenge, from models to production.

14
00:00:52,104 --> 00:00:56,321
So banks and lenders, they hold
mortgages, they hold commercial

15
00:00:56,326 --> 00:01:02,031
loans and other lending assets that
don't have a quoted market price.

16
00:01:02,346 --> 00:01:07,369
Like a stock will have a quoted market
price, but, these mortgages will not.

17
00:01:07,902 --> 00:01:09,548
You can't look up their value.

18
00:01:09,939 --> 00:01:15,615
You actually have to model it and
keep remodeling it as rates, spreads,

19
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:17,612
and borrower behavior changes.

20
00:01:18,278 --> 00:01:23,564
So the models themselves are often quite
sophisticated, but in my experience,

21
00:01:23,896 --> 00:01:28,161
getting them into production reliably
is a very hard problem to solve.

22
00:01:29,009 --> 00:01:30,376
Now, I have two points here.

23
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:34,597
First, accuracy alone is insufficient.

24
00:01:35,052 --> 00:01:41,387
A great model that can be run
consistently at scale isn't useful.

25
00:01:42,071 --> 00:01:45,151
Second, auditability is non-negotiable.

26
00:01:45,773 --> 00:01:50,167
Regulators expect every
valuation to be explainable and

27
00:01:50,169 --> 00:01:53,192
traceable back to its inputs.

28
00:01:54,594 --> 00:01:58,195
So our agenda for today.

29
00:01:58,728 --> 00:02:00,482
So here's how we will go.

30
00:02:00,673 --> 00:02:05,345
First, the pipeline architecture,
how market data, scenarios, and

31
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:07,333
cash flow models fit together.

32
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:12,364
Second, versioning and
reproducibility, how to rerun

33
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:14,461
any historical valuation exactly.

34
00:02:15,070 --> 00:02:20,050
Third, observability and controls,
which includes testing, lineage,

35
00:02:20,075 --> 00:02:22,040
failure handling, and validation.

36
00:02:22,495 --> 00:02:27,072
And finally, event-driven processing,
how to read, market changes

37
00:02:27,079 --> 00:02:29,217
without recomputing everything.

38
00:02:30,059 --> 00:02:35,903
So let's, start with the
pipeline architecture.

39
00:02:36,903 --> 00:02:37,243
Okay.

40
00:02:37,884 --> 00:02:41,485
at its core, the pipeline
has three stages.

41
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:45,060
Market data ingestion comes first.

42
00:02:45,468 --> 00:02:47,421
That's everything the model depends on.

43
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:52,766
The scenario setup, where we decide
which economic assumptions to apply,

44
00:02:53,254 --> 00:02:57,392
and finally, cash flow modeling,
where we project payments and

45
00:02:57,392 --> 00:02:59,345
discount them to a present value.

46
00:03:00,196 --> 00:03:05,109
The principle that matters most,
each stage must be independently

47
00:03:05,126 --> 00:03:10,681
versioned, testable, and auditable,
and failures must be isolated.

48
00:03:11,294 --> 00:03:14,021
A bad input should stop the pipeline.

49
00:03:14,447 --> 00:03:18,314
It should never be silently
passed downstream into a number

50
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,670
someone signs off on Okay.

51
00:03:22,055 --> 00:03:25,926
So now market data ingestion.

52
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:32,106
So every downstream depends on market
data, so treat it as first-class

53
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:34,417
artifact with full lineage.

54
00:03:35,306 --> 00:03:40,261
In practice, we ingest rate curves,
credit spreads, and prepayment

55
00:03:40,286 --> 00:03:42,559
indices for multiple providers.

56
00:03:43,057 --> 00:03:47,544
Every ingestion batch gets a
timestamp and a hash, so we can prove

57
00:03:47,545 --> 00:03:50,524
exactly which data a valuation used.

58
00:03:50,886 --> 00:03:56,921
We run schema validation and anomaly
detection right at the boundary before

59
00:03:56,939 --> 00:04:02,794
bad data gets in, and anything rejected
or malformed goes to a dead letter queue,

60
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:13,170
so it's quarantined and reviewable, not
quietly lost Okay, moving on to Cash

61
00:04:13,182 --> 00:04:16,211
flow, configurable economic, scenarios.

62
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,332
So next is scenario management.

63
00:04:18,733 --> 00:04:23,762
The goal is to separate economic
assumptions from model logic.

64
00:04:24,168 --> 00:04:28,290
Team needs to run a base case,
stress cases, and regulatory

65
00:04:28,291 --> 00:04:32,645
scenarios against the same cash flow
engine without changing any code.

66
00:04:33,222 --> 00:04:35,094
So we have three building blocks here.

67
00:04:35,505 --> 00:04:40,578
A scenario registry where each
scenario is named, versioned, and

68
00:04:40,578 --> 00:04:42,990
stored as an immutable artifact.

69
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:48,746
Parameterized runs where scenario
overrides are injected when the pipeline

70
00:04:48,764 --> 00:04:51,040
is invoked and never hard-coded.

71
00:04:51,472 --> 00:04:56,280
And parallel execution fanning out
across scenarios at the same time,

72
00:04:56,641 --> 00:05:02,424
which is faster and guarantees
that all ran on identical inputs.

73
00:05:03,769 --> 00:05:09,945
Okay, so next is, cash flow
modeling and discounting.

74
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:14,960
So essentially, the heart of the
system is the cash flow engine

75
00:05:15,522 --> 00:05:17,770
with four design principles.

76
00:05:18,517 --> 00:05:23,058
The first one is the engine itself,
which is stateless and deterministic.

77
00:05:23,674 --> 00:05:27,522
Identical inputs, identical
outputs every time.

78
00:05:28,147 --> 00:05:32,109
Second is prepayment and
credit models are pluggable.

79
00:05:32,602 --> 00:05:36,091
You can swap one out without
rewiring the pipeline.

80
00:05:36,614 --> 00:05:42,188
And third is the discount curves are
injected, not embedded in model logic.

81
00:05:42,863 --> 00:05:46,990
And finally, the fourth one,
we output the intermediate cash

82
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:49,791
flows, not just the final NPV.

83
00:05:50,149 --> 00:05:55,830
When a value moves, those intermediate
numbers are how you explain why.

84
00:05:57,405 --> 00:06:04,020
Okay, so we will move on to
chapter two, which is our

85
00:06:04,115 --> 00:06:06,772
versioning and reproducibility.

86
00:06:07,687 --> 00:06:08,724
Okay, let's begin.

87
00:06:09,597 --> 00:06:14,097
So versioning essentially
has three layers.

88
00:06:14,964 --> 00:06:19,704
The first one is the data version,
which includes the market data snapshot,

89
00:06:20,196 --> 00:06:22,547
portfolio positions, and reference data.

90
00:06:23,279 --> 00:06:27,576
Now, another form of versioning is
assumption versioning, which includes

91
00:06:27,608 --> 00:06:32,094
prepayment speeds, credit loss
curves, discount rate conventions.

92
00:06:32,398 --> 00:06:37,677
And finally, model config versions,
which is algorithmic parameters,

93
00:06:37,871 --> 00:06:40,519
feature sets, and calibration output.

94
00:06:40,984 --> 00:06:41,998
The rule is simple.

95
00:06:42,539 --> 00:06:47,801
Every valuation run ID must pin a
specific version in all three layers.

96
00:06:48,275 --> 00:06:52,138
Then you can reproduce any
historical valuation just by

97
00:06:52,371 --> 00:06:55,373
replaying its run manifest.

98
00:06:56,994 --> 00:06:57,652
Okay.

99
00:06:58,467 --> 00:07:03,592
Now I'll discuss about
reproducing historical valuations.

100
00:07:03,999 --> 00:07:07,395
So why does, reproducibility
break in practice?

101
00:07:08,265 --> 00:07:11,297
Usually it's, due to
one of the four things.

102
00:07:12,357 --> 00:07:14,921
reference data gets overwritten in place.

103
00:07:15,488 --> 00:07:18,971
Model parameters live only
in someone's notebook.

104
00:07:19,294 --> 00:07:22,359
Someone make a manual override
to an assumption and doesn't

105
00:07:22,361 --> 00:07:27,662
document it, or a dependency gets
upgraded in the middle of a cycle.

106
00:07:28,294 --> 00:07:30,776
The fix is an immutable run manifest.

107
00:07:31,277 --> 00:07:35,806
Every run emits a signed
manifest with data snapshot IDs.

108
00:07:36,295 --> 00:07:40,579
The assumption set hash, the
model config version, and a hash

109
00:07:40,583 --> 00:07:44,406
of the pipeline itself, and a
digest of execution environment.

110
00:07:45,089 --> 00:07:49,271
And critically, store manifests
in append-only storage.

111
00:07:49,329 --> 00:07:50,506
Never mutate them.

112
00:07:50,972 --> 00:07:54,600
That manifest is your audit defense

113
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:59,005
Next up, I want to cover,
safe model updates.

114
00:07:59,298 --> 00:08:06,390
So models evolve over time, and updating
a live valuation system is pretty risky.

115
00:08:06,596 --> 00:08:08,861
So you need a structured promotion path.

116
00:08:09,578 --> 00:08:11,748
So essentially, it
starts in a shadow mode.

117
00:08:12,174 --> 00:08:17,885
The new model runs in parallel on
real data, but its output is not used.

118
00:08:18,399 --> 00:08:23,754
Then a challenger test, which is
head-to-head against the current baseline.

119
00:08:24,313 --> 00:08:28,316
Then a staged rolled out to a
subset of the portfolio, and

120
00:08:28,332 --> 00:08:30,477
only then a full cut over.

121
00:08:30,977 --> 00:08:36,293
At every step, rollback must be a single
command with zero manual intervention.

122
00:08:36,831 --> 00:08:42,182
If going back is hard, people hesitate,
and that's when problem starts.

123
00:08:44,053 --> 00:08:44,489
Okay.

124
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:50,608
So now we'll, discuss more about,
observability and operational

125
00:08:50,628 --> 00:08:53,097
controls, which is my chapter three.

126
00:08:53,762 --> 00:08:57,617
So reproducibility tells you
what happened after the fact.

127
00:08:58,033 --> 00:09:01,762
Observability and controls tells
you something wrong before a

128
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:03,921
bad number goes out the door.

129
00:09:05,465 --> 00:09:05,825
Okay.

130
00:09:06,526 --> 00:09:10,952
So what should you instrument?

131
00:09:11,154 --> 00:09:15,028
Basically, there are four categories
which we should be worried about.

132
00:09:15,990 --> 00:09:21,259
the first one is the execution metrics,
which includes stage latency, throughput,

133
00:09:21,539 --> 00:09:27,193
retry rates, and at the end, this,
tells us, is the pipeline healthy?

134
00:09:27,914 --> 00:09:32,589
Now, the second metrics could be data
quality, which includes null rates,

135
00:09:33,001 --> 00:09:35,686
out-of-range values, distribution drift.

136
00:09:36,359 --> 00:09:39,717
in this, the question really
is, are the inputs healthy?

137
00:09:40,724 --> 00:09:46,562
Now, the third metrics is model output,
which is actually the shifts in the

138
00:09:46,583 --> 00:09:49,661
NPV distribution spread anomalies.

139
00:09:50,115 --> 00:09:53,560
And the question we ask here is,
do the result look plausible?

140
00:09:54,515 --> 00:09:59,495
And business rule checks, which includes
portfolio-level constraints, validated

141
00:09:59,495 --> 00:10:01,218
before any result is published.

142
00:10:01,686 --> 00:10:03,814
That last one is the most important.

143
00:10:04,316 --> 00:10:07,503
It's the final gate between
the pipeline and the people

144
00:10:07,511 --> 00:10:11,598
who rely on its numbers Okay.

145
00:10:11,941 --> 00:10:15,390
Now, failure handling and data lineage.

146
00:10:15,934 --> 00:10:19,887
So pipeline fails, that's
for sure and that's given.

147
00:10:20,373 --> 00:10:24,229
The question is whether
failures are cheap and safe.

148
00:10:24,853 --> 00:10:26,542
Three patterns can help us here.

149
00:10:27,748 --> 00:10:29,188
Idempotent stages.

150
00:10:29,644 --> 00:10:32,341
Identical input and identical output.

151
00:10:33,227 --> 00:10:38,207
So retries are safe if we have identical
input and as I said, identical output

152
00:10:38,548 --> 00:10:40,980
with no side effects or duplicate writes.

153
00:10:41,765 --> 00:10:42,963
Checkpoint and resume.

154
00:10:43,763 --> 00:10:49,221
Save outputs at each stage boundary, so
a failed run resumes from the last clean

155
00:10:49,221 --> 00:10:51,369
checkpoint instead of starting over.

156
00:10:52,027 --> 00:10:55,741
And full data lineage tracking
every transformation from

157
00:10:55,742 --> 00:10:57,346
source to final valuation.

158
00:10:57,884 --> 00:11:02,544
You should be able to ask which run
consumed this version of the rate

159
00:11:02,547 --> 00:11:04,803
curve and get an answer in seconds.

160
00:11:05,241 --> 00:11:11,679
When a vendor tells you a curve was
wrong, that question matters a lot Okay.

161
00:11:12,064 --> 00:11:15,619
next is, automated testing
for valuation pipelines.

162
00:11:16,721 --> 00:11:20,239
So valuation pipeline needs
four layers of testing.

163
00:11:20,699 --> 00:11:26,179
Unit test for code logic, which is
your cash flow calculations, prepayment

164
00:11:26,179 --> 00:11:28,412
formulas, discounting conventions.

165
00:11:28,957 --> 00:11:33,699
Integration tests that run the
pipeline end-to-end with synthetic loan

166
00:11:33,733 --> 00:11:36,480
portfolios and controlled market data.

167
00:11:37,216 --> 00:11:42,186
Regression tests that compare output
against pinned historical baselines

168
00:11:42,251 --> 00:11:47,368
after any change, so you know
if a code change moved a number.

169
00:11:47,729 --> 00:11:53,019
And stress test with extreme scenario
inputs to confirm the system stays

170
00:11:53,085 --> 00:11:55,762
numerically stable at the boundaries.

171
00:11:56,762 --> 00:12:00,038
next is model validation and auditability.

172
00:12:00,702 --> 00:12:05,389
So in regulated finance, someone
independent will check your work,

173
00:12:05,603 --> 00:12:07,502
so the idea is to build for it.

174
00:12:08,308 --> 00:12:13,897
Independent validation teams need access
to run manifest and intermediate output.

175
00:12:14,831 --> 00:12:18,900
Challenger model comparison should
be documented in the model risk

176
00:12:18,900 --> 00:12:23,484
framework, and assumption sensitivity
reports should be generated

177
00:12:23,981 --> 00:12:26,263
automatically on every production run.

178
00:12:27,098 --> 00:12:32,736
On the right is the audit pack of
every run, the signed manifest, a

179
00:12:32,736 --> 00:12:38,655
lineage graph report, intermediate
cash flow snapshot, output distribution

180
00:12:38,662 --> 00:12:43,721
statistics, the business rule
validation report, and approval record.

181
00:12:44,535 --> 00:12:51,198
The broader point here is regulators
increasingly expect MLOps-grade controls.

182
00:12:51,675 --> 00:12:57,811
Versioned models, documented assumptions,
and reproducible outputs aren't just good

183
00:12:57,849 --> 00:13:01,579
practice, they are examination targets.

184
00:13:03,536 --> 00:13:10,570
Okay, this takes us to our chapter
four, which is event-driven processing,

185
00:13:10,578 --> 00:13:14,883
where we are going to talk about
scale and how to stop recomputing

186
00:13:15,350 --> 00:13:18,992
everything every time something changes.

187
00:13:19,409 --> 00:13:20,796
So here we go

188
00:13:21,796 --> 00:13:26,114
Most valuation systems revalue
the whole portfolio on a schedule.

189
00:13:26,855 --> 00:13:32,611
That works, but it's expensive
and slow, and most of the compute

190
00:13:32,666 --> 00:13:35,772
actually is spent on loans where
nothing is actually changing.

191
00:13:36,396 --> 00:13:39,058
Event-driven patterns flip that around.

192
00:13:39,605 --> 00:13:43,059
You revalue only the segments
affected by a change.

193
00:13:43,658 --> 00:13:48,027
A rate curve update revalue
just the rates and RV tranches.

194
00:13:48,744 --> 00:13:54,282
A position change revalue just the
impacted loans and an assumption

195
00:13:54,287 --> 00:14:00,500
update, a scope rerun with lineage
tracked so you can always see which

196
00:14:00,503 --> 00:14:02,116
numbers were refreshed and why.

197
00:14:02,648 --> 00:14:07,771
This is where the earlier pieces pay
off because every input is versioned

198
00:14:07,851 --> 00:14:09,518
and every dependency is tracked.

199
00:14:09,880 --> 00:14:13,422
The system knows exactly
what needs recomputing.

200
00:14:13,824 --> 00:14:17,560
Lower compute cost, much lower latency

201
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:18,941
Okay.

202
00:14:19,319 --> 00:14:24,796
Now I want to briefly touch base
some key architectural principles.

203
00:14:25,084 --> 00:14:30,220
So if I boil the architecture
down, it rests on four principles.

204
00:14:30,767 --> 00:14:32,915
Immutability as a foundation.

205
00:14:33,374 --> 00:14:38,887
This is our data assumptions and
configs are version and append only

206
00:14:39,316 --> 00:14:44,302
because mutation is the root cause
of most reproducibility failures.

207
00:14:44,883 --> 00:14:46,472
Observability by design.

208
00:14:47,065 --> 00:14:50,840
Instrument every stage from
day one, so problems surface

209
00:14:50,863 --> 00:14:52,941
before they reach stakeholders.

210
00:14:53,424 --> 00:14:59,104
Testability at every layer with pipeline
changes gated on a automated test passing.

211
00:14:59,691 --> 00:15:01,700
And auditability as a feature.

212
00:15:02,094 --> 00:15:08,152
Regulatory traceability as a product
requirement built into core, not bolted on

213
00:15:09,152 --> 00:15:09,488
Okay.

214
00:15:09,740 --> 00:15:13,304
Now, some of the key takeaways.

215
00:15:13,323 --> 00:15:16,207
So three things actually I
would like you to take away.

216
00:15:16,657 --> 00:15:21,563
First, in regulated valuation
systems, reliability, traceability,

217
00:15:21,566 --> 00:15:24,871
and reproducibility aren't
optional engineering quality.

218
00:15:25,235 --> 00:15:27,836
They are core MLOps requirements.

219
00:15:28,386 --> 00:15:32,463
Second, versioning across
data, assumptions, and model

220
00:15:32,467 --> 00:15:34,158
config is the foundation.

221
00:15:34,554 --> 00:15:38,711
Without it, you can't
investigate a historical number

222
00:15:38,796 --> 00:15:40,394
or defend it in an audit.

223
00:15:40,912 --> 00:15:45,519
And third, event-driven architecture
lets you respond to market and

224
00:15:45,528 --> 00:15:50,021
portfolio changes quickly at a
scale without recomputing the whole

225
00:15:50,410 --> 00:15:56,303
portfolio every time Finally, I
want to say thank you for your time.

226
00:15:56,330 --> 00:16:00,001
I hope this gives you a practical
blueprint for running valuation

227
00:16:00,011 --> 00:16:04,284
models with same rigor we expect
from the model themselves.

228
00:16:04,319 --> 00:16:07,812
I'll be glad to continue the
conversation, so feel free to reach out.

229
00:16:08,330 --> 00:16:09,299
Thank you so much.

